Save93 equipment runs comparing "skip blocked" alone against "read-only overtake" (VMCatchUp::SetSkipBlocked(true) + VMCatchUp::SetHoldChangeCalls(true)), 2026-11-11 03:48-03:59 / 2026-11-10 22:46 build / AE 1.6.1170 / limiter 30 FPS, DynamicUpdateBudget=false / Papyrus bEnableLogging=0, bEnableProfiling=1
Test DLL APBGameplayTrace.dll sha256 972440fc6cc62fabbb0c51519e28e11f52027ebeb2e0f34dc61ab827b311ec90 (release DLL of the same source 30e58cbb..., which never calls either test control). APB_CATCHUP_MODE=accelerate, target 60, engage below 50, reviewed-read rule with property reads.
  shc-30-1, shc-30-2   catchup-holdcalls with -SkipBlocked: catch-up and skip-blocked are ON in every trial; "off" = HoldChangeCalls(false) (skip blocked alone, first unreviewed/mutating call of a stack may advance and marks the stack changed), "on" = HoldChangeCalls(true) (read-only overtake: only reviewed reads and auto-property getters may overtake blocked calls in the queue; any queued call judged a change is itself held).
No crash while the trials ran; guard_violations and overrun_pauses 0 in all 16 trials; no "turned off" warning; all 16 trials matched 12 of 12 equipment requests within 250 ms; foreground_pct 100.0 in all 16.

RESULT, drain of the equipment handlers (ms after the last signal; trials with the same handler count):
  174 handlers: skip alone ("off") 258, 457, 519, 523, 557, 624 (6 trials, mean 490) / skip + hold-change-calls ("on") 820, 822, 823, 922 (4 trials, mean 847, +72.9% vs skip alone; -36.6% vs skfx catch-up off 1335, -25.4% vs gab release rule 1135).
  202-203 handlers: skip alone ("off") 724, 857 (2 trials, mean 790) / skip + hold-change-calls ("on") 1354, 1356, 1389, 1557 (4 trials, mean 1414, +79.0% vs skip alone; -29.3% vs skfx catch-up off 2001, -23.1% vs gab release rule 1840).
RESULT, queue wait of every queued native call of every script (verification/readlist-review/all_waits.py, below):
  skip alone ("off"): 21.92 and 21.96 ms (same-frame share 35.5% and 34.7%, two_plus 2.1% and 1.8%).
  skip + hold-change-calls ("on"): 29.33 and 29.10 ms (same-frame share 28.0% and 27.8%, two_plus 4.8% and 4.5%) -- back to the non-skipping release rule range (gab on: 28.24 and 31.38 ms).
RESULT, order restoration:
  skip alone ("off"): 11 order_rebuilds across 1,898 skip cycles (per trial: 2, 0, 4, 0 / 2, 1, 2, 0).
  skip + hold-change-calls ("on"): 0 order_rebuilds across all 1,781 skip cycles (0 in all 8 trials), confirming mid-cycle queue insertions only happen when unreviewed/mutating first calls execute during an extra cycle.
COST: extra time per frame 0.12-0.14 ms ("off", max single frame 0.86 ms) and 0.09-0.12 ms ("on", max single frame 0.84 ms); change_call_holds 133-419 per "on" trial; front_skips 926-1491 ("off") vs 1146-1956 ("on"). Engine frame time p99 36.06-36.13 ms ("off") / 36.12-36.22 ms ("on"). Utility.Wait response p95 58.1-58.7 ms ("off") / 58.2-58.4 ms ("on").
CHECKED (2026-11-11, Claude session): the runs were started by Gemini (Antigravity) through its local runner; every figure above was recomputed from build/catchup/shc-30-1 and shc-30-2 (tests/analyze_catchup_ab.py, all_waits.py) and agrees. Three limits on the reading below: (1) "skip alone" against "skip + hold-change-calls" is the only comparison made inside one launch; the percentages against catch-up off (skfx, same build, other launch) and against the release rule (gab, 17:26 build, other launch) are indirect. (2) 0 order_rebuilds in 8 trials agrees with the explanation given in the RESULT line but does not prove that insertions come only from change calls. (3) A read that overtakes is followed by its script's bytecode, which may write properties or call tasklet-callable natives that change state, so the order between scripts is still not the stock order. Both launches left a crash log when the game was closed (8 s and 7 s after the last trial; execute access violation, no module of this project in the stack scan; the first scan shows PrismaUI and SKSEMenuFramework, the second the input-hook modules of the earlier exit crashes).
READING: read-only overtake eliminates mutating native overtakes and mid-cycle queue rebuilds (order_rebuilds = 0) and still shortens equipment handler drain by ~29-37% vs stock (and ~23-25% vs the non-skipping release rule), because reviewed reads and property getters no longer stall behind an unrelated script at the queue front. However, holding every unlisted query/timer call (e.g., RegisterForSingleUpdate, UI.IsMenuOpen) breaks each handler's own call chain (extra_calls 689-1007 -> 514-841), giving back ~43% of the equipment drain speedup vs skip alone and returning global mean queue wait (29.1-29.3 ms) to the non-skipping release-rule level.

